<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Millicent Garrett Fawcett</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Millicent Fawcett</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Millicent Garrett Fawcett was an English political activist and writer who led the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s largest women's rights association, from 1897 to 1919. She campaigned for the vote through changes in the law and distanced herself from the militancy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-social-and-political-union">Women's Social and Political Union</a>. She co-founded what became Newnham College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1871, and in 1901 led a commission of women sent to investigate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> of the South African War. In 2018 she became the first woman honoured with a statue in Parliament Square, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millicent-garrett-fawcett.jpg" alt="Millicent Garrett Fawcett" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Millicent_Fawcett.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Millicent Garrett was born on 11 June 1847 in Aldeburgh, the eighth of ten children of Newson Garrett, a businessman from nearby Leiston, and his wife Louisa. The family encouraged children to read widely and to share their father's political interests. Her elder sister Elizabeth Garrett Anderson, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first female doctor, introduced her to the suffragist Emily Davies, who told her that she was the younger and must attend to the vote.</p><p>In 1865 she heard a lecture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>. Through Mill she met Henry Fawcett, a Liberal Member of Parliament who had been blinded in a shooting accident in 1858. They married on 23 April 1867, and she acted as his secretary while building a writing career. Their only child, Philippa, was born in 1868.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fawcett joined the London Suffrage Committee in 1868 and spoke at the first public pro-suffrage meeting in London in 1869. Her Political Economy for Beginners (1870) ran to ten editions in forty-one years. With Anne Clough she helped found Newnham Hall in 1871. After Henry died on 6 November 1884 she withdrew briefly from public life, then returned in 1885. She joined the Liberal Unionist Party in 1886 to oppose Irish Home Rule, and resigned from the Unionists in 1904 over free trade.</p><p>After the death of Lydia Becker she led the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, as its president by 1897. She kept to argument and legal change and distanced herself from the militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-social-and-political-union">Women's Social and Political Union</a>, which she believed would sour public opinion and alienate members of Parliament. By 1905 the NUWSS had 305 constituent societies and almost 50,000 members. It held its first large open-air procession, the Mud March, on 9 February 1907.</p><p>In July 1901 she sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> to lead a commission of women investigating conditions in the camps where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> families were interned. She also campaigned to raise the age of consent and to repeal the Contagious Diseases Acts, which she saw as built on a sexual double standard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The NUWSS kept campaigning during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and Fawcett held her post until 1919, a year after women first received the vote under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/representation-of-the-people-act-1918">Representation of the People Act 1918</a>. She was appointed Dame Grand Cross of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in 1925 and died at her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> home in Gower Street on 5 August 1929. A memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, unveiled in 1932, reads: A wise constant and courageous Englishwoman. She won citizenship for women. In 2018 a statue by Gillian Wearing was unveiled in Parliament Square, after a campaign led by Caroline Criado Perez that gathered over 84,000 signatures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Millicent Fawcett” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Britain, Lydia Becker of Manchester led the early societies and <b>Millicent Garrett Fawcett</b> the non-militant National Union from 1897 to 1919.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies had taken it up in 1917, and <b>Millicent Fawcett</b> asked to use it at a Suffrage Demonstration Concert on 13 March 1918; Parry orchestrated it for the occasion and agreed that it should become the Women Voters' Hymn, assigning the copyright…</blockquote><a href="/songs/and-did-those-feet-in-ancient-time">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aldeburgh, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Gower Street, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.15,1.6&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Aldeburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Millicent Garrett Fawcett"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5084 5668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5084 5668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.15,1.6&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If the nineteenth century was a time of education for women, it was no less a time of education for men.&#8221;</p><footer>Women's Suffrage: A Short History of a Great Movement (1912) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48614/pg48614-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When a fire is once kindled many things will serve as fuel which to a superficial glance would seem to have no connection with it.&#8221;</p><footer>Women's Suffrage: A Short History of a Great Movement (1912) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48614/pg48614-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
